00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.389 align:start position:0%
 
just look at this  there's something truly magical about  the images of award-winning photographer  frank  relley through his lens trees houses and  other common objects  are transformed into vivid even haunting  scenes  works that now have a place in both  private collections and prestigious  museums  including the smithsonian our jamie wax  joined the new orleans native at his  gallery  and out on the water to observe the  painstaking process of capturing these  stunning portraits  of the world around us photographs  are a powerful way of of communicating  for people  and have been and i think they will  continue to be for a long time  there are many photographers who focus  on human subjects to express  themes such as connection loneliness  aging and loss frank really manages to  express  all those human themes and more in his  photographs  without ever actually featuring a single  human  moments like today you know when we you  know i have a chance to zoom out and  reflect and i'm not in the midst of it  all  i realize it's like oh wow this all  these  pieces of my life have come together and  it feels amazing  the louisiana native has been  photographing landscapes  natural and man-made for 17 years  when one of his photographs was featured  as the cover image of dan baum's 2009  best-selling book about hurricane  katrina  nine lives more people began to take  interest in relly's work  what impact did that have on  your art and your ability to connect  with a wider audience  for me it was very powerful to have the  image on the outside of that book  uh speak to the lives that were on the  inside of it it's super special because  ultimately i'm trying to get people's  attention  through the images and so when it goes  out as broadly as that then it's super  powerful  reli's artistic focus now is mainly on  louisiana's bayous and swamps  utilizing evocative lighting and long  exposure times  he creates stunning and emotional images  your work evolved from these dramatic  photographs  of architectural structures in louisiana  to nature scenes swamp scenes isolated  trees  that evolution happened in multiple  different ways after i've been  photographing the architecture in new  orleans for a while  i wanted to get back to my love of the  natural world but i didn't know the path  to it  hurricane katrina of course happened and  that was a huge water story how water  came into the city of new orleans  and then the disaster with the oil spill  in the gulf of mexico  that was another story that was  connected to the water both of these  were  dramatic photographic events that  captured all of our attention  and i i just realized that in louisiana  all of the stories  come from the water the photographs are  made of  you know natural subjects they're made  of trees and they're made of water and  they're made of skies and stars but  they're made for people  and the most profound way that we  connect with  people is through relationships and  something we can all understand and we  all  understand the shape of an embrace  they become something that has a human  connection in it  relly's former studio director shimsi  frazelle  believes that human connection has the  power to reflect both our pessimism  and our optimism sometimes together  i think that frank through his work has  never  really shied away from the potential for  tragedy and what that can do to  an environment what that can do to  someone's spirit but i think through his  technique  as a photographer what his work has  always done for me  is sort of blended the reality  of potential tragedy with hope  a lot of his photographs are shot in  remote areas  at night a place he says fear and  possibility  coexist the process is intense  at times with rallying up to 25 feet of  water for  hours at a time and he's not usually  alone you have been in situations where  15 to  20 alligators are surrounding you in  maybe 16 feet of water  and you're right there yes and you say  you're not afraid of that situation  no i know that people in those  situations  you know i know that people are afraid  but i typically feel very calm in the  natural world and that calm  you know being down in the water despite  the fact that there are alligators in  the water  is a calming experience for me i am  stuck in swamp mud  i have no idea how you do this it was  something i just  had to experience firsthand are you  looking for humanity  in these things that you shoot sometimes  it's a feeling that you get from the  tree in the middle of the night  the only souls for miles deep  in moropa swamp in freezing water  sinking in quicksand mud filled with  alligators in a hibernation-like state  it was frank relley's happy place  how's that feel that feels great you  have a method of  treading water and floating so that you  don't disturb that  that long exposure how important is  lighting  in the emotional content that you  capture lighting is everything  i mean lighting is the language it's the  music  of of photography you know there's the  what you're photographing there's the  subject  and the light is the feel you know being  able to to work at night and  you know in shape light to create that  feeling it's it's the key  and to know frank relly and his work  is to know that the light doesn't just  come from without  but also within i look at your smile  right now i mean you are really living  your boyhood dream in a lot of ways i  completely am  and i'm i i feel so lucky for that it's  amazing  finding that path has been you know full  of challenges and hurdles and right now  in this moment i feel  really grateful for you know being able  to make my way there  and excited about what comes next for  cbs this morning saturday  jamie wax new orleans he seems so  delightful and i tell you he's bringing  to light  the conditions of the cypress swamp so  many cypress trees  are endangered now so i know that those  pictures  people are going to buy them and perhaps  they'll tell a story  they're beautiful yeah i mean they're  just stunning images  really and we all know lighting good  lighting he's right odds are all about  that lighting yeah

